# Dockerfile Explanation – Telemetry Documentation Service

This Dockerfile is used to generate, build, and serve the **Telemetry Documentation Website**.

The service creates documentation from the OpenTelemetry telemetry schema and publishes it as a static website.

It uses a **three-stage build**:

1. Generate documentation using Weaver
2. Build a static website using MkDocs
3. Serve the website using Nginx

---

# What Does This Service Do?

The Telemetry Documentation Service:

* Reads telemetry schema definitions
* Generates Markdown documentation automatically
* Builds a documentation website
* Serves the website through Nginx
* Makes telemetry information easily accessible through a browser

---

# Complete Build Flow

```text
Telemetry Schema
       │
       ▼
Weaver
(Generate Markdown)
       │
       ▼
MkDocs
(Build Static Website)
       │
       ▼
Nginx
(Serve Website)
       │
       ▼
Users Access Documentation
```

---

# Stage 1 – Generate Documentation Using Weaver

The first stage creates documentation files from the telemetry schema.

---

## Step 1: Use Weaver Image

```dockerfile
FROM docker.io/otel/weaver:v0.22.1 AS registry-builder
```

### Purpose

Uses the Weaver tool.

### What is Weaver?

Weaver is an OpenTelemetry documentation generator.

It can:

* Read telemetry schemas
* Generate documentation automatically
* Create Markdown pages
* Create documentation navigation files

---

## Step 2: Set Working Directory

```dockerfile
WORKDIR /workspace
```

### Purpose

Sets:

```text
/workspace
```

as the working directory.

All files are processed from here.

---

## Step 3: Copy Telemetry Schema

```dockerfile
COPY telemetry-schema /workspace/telemetry-schema
```

### Purpose

Copies the telemetry schema into the container.

### What is a Telemetry Schema?

A schema defines:

* Services
* Metrics
* Traces
* Attributes
* Events

Example:

```text
Cart Service
Checkout Service
Payment Service
Recommendation Service
```

along with their telemetry definitions.

---

## Step 4: Copy Weaver Templates

```dockerfile
COPY src/telemetry-docs/templates /workspace/templates
```

### Purpose

Copies custom documentation templates.

Templates control:

* Page layout
* Formatting
* Navigation structure
* Generated content style

---

## Step 5: Generate Documentation Pages

```dockerfile
RUN /weaver/weaver registry generate \
    --registry=/workspace/telemetry-schema \
    --templates=/workspace/templates \
    markdown \
    /workspace/docs
```

### Purpose

Generates Markdown documentation.

### Input

```text
Telemetry Schema
+
Templates
```

### Output

```text
/workspace/docs
```

containing:

```text
Service Pages
Metric Pages
Attribute Pages
Documentation Navigation
mkdocs.yml
```

---

## Step 6: Generate Packaged Schema

```dockerfile
RUN /weaver/weaver registry package \
    --registry=/workspace/telemetry-schema \
    --resolved-schema-uri=https://opentelemetry.io/schemas/1.40.0 \
    --output=/workspace/docs/schema \
    --v2
```

### Purpose

Creates a packaged version of the telemetry schema.

### Why?

Allows users to:

* Download schema files
* View resolved schema definitions
* Reference schema information

---

### Output Location

```text
/workspace/docs/schema
```

---

# Stage 2 – Build Documentation Website

The second stage converts Markdown files into a static website.

---

## Step 7: Use Python Image

```dockerfile
FROM python:3.14-slim-bookworm AS site-builder
```

### Purpose

Uses a lightweight Python image.

### Why Python?

MkDocs is a Python-based documentation tool.

---

## Step 8: Set Working Directory

```dockerfile
WORKDIR /app
```

### Purpose

Sets:

```text
/app
```

as the working directory.

---

## Step 9: Install MkDocs

```dockerfile
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir mkdocs mkdocs-material
```

### Purpose

Installs website generation tools.

---

### MkDocs

Creates documentation websites from Markdown files.

---

### MkDocs Material

Provides:

* Modern design
* Better navigation
* Search functionality
* Responsive UI

---

## Step 10: Copy Base Configuration

```dockerfile
COPY src/telemetry-docs/mkdocs.yml /app/mkdocs-base.yml
```

### Purpose

Copies the MkDocs configuration file.

This file defines:

* Site title
* Theme
* Navigation
* Extensions

---

## Step 11: Copy Generated Documentation

```dockerfile
COPY --from=registry-builder /workspace/docs /app/docs
```

### Purpose

Copies documentation generated by Weaver.

---

## Step 12: Build Website

```dockerfile
RUN mv /app/docs/mkdocs.yml /app/mkdocs.yml && \
    mkdocs build --clean
```

### What Happens?

#### Move Configuration

```dockerfile
mv /app/docs/mkdocs.yml /app/mkdocs.yml
```

Moves generated MkDocs configuration to the expected location.

---

#### Build Website

```dockerfile
mkdocs build --clean
```

Converts:

```text
Markdown Files
```

into:

```text
HTML
CSS
JavaScript
```

website files.

---

### Output

```text
/app/site
```

This folder contains the complete static website.

---

# Stage 3 – Serve Website Using Nginx

The final stage serves the documentation website.

---

## Step 13: Use Nginx Image

```dockerfile
FROM docker.io/nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged:1.29-alpine3.23-otel
```

### Purpose

Uses Nginx to serve the website.

### Benefits

* Fast
* Lightweight
* Production-ready
* Supports OpenTelemetry integration

---

## Step 14: Run as Non-Root User

```dockerfile
USER 101
```

### Purpose

Runs Nginx using a non-root user.

### Benefits

* Better security
* Reduced permissions
* Safer deployments

---

## Step 15: Copy Website Files

```dockerfile
COPY --from=site-builder /app/site /static
```

### Purpose

Copies the generated website into Nginx.

Result:

```text
/static
```

contains:

```text
HTML files
CSS files
JavaScript files
Images
Search index
```

---

## Step 16: Copy Nginx Template

```dockerfile
COPY src/telemetry-docs/nginx.conf.template /nginx.conf.template
```

### Purpose

Copies the Nginx configuration template.

This template contains placeholders for environment variables.

---

## Step 17: Expose Service Port

```dockerfile
EXPOSE ${TELEMETRY_DOCS_PORT}
```

### Purpose

Documents which port Nginx listens on.

Example:

```bash
TELEMETRY_DOCS_PORT=8080
```

Website becomes available on:

```text
http://server-ip:8080
```

---

## Step 18: Define Stop Signal

```dockerfile
STOPSIGNAL SIGQUIT
```

### Purpose

Allows Nginx to shut down gracefully.

### Benefit

Active requests can finish before stopping.

---

## Step 19: Start Nginx

```dockerfile
CMD ["/bin/sh" , "-c" , "envsubst '$OTEL_COLLECTOR_HOST $OTEL_COLLECTOR_PORT_GRPC $OTEL_SERVICE_NAME $TELEMETRY_DOCS_PORT' < /nginx.conf.template > /etc/nginx/nginx.conf && exec nginx -g 'daemon off;'"]
```

### What Happens?

#### Step 1

Read:

```text
nginx.conf.template
```

---

#### Step 2

Replace environment variables:

```text
$OTEL_COLLECTOR_HOST
$OTEL_COLLECTOR_PORT_GRPC
$OTEL_SERVICE_NAME
$TELEMETRY_DOCS_PORT
```

with actual values.

---

#### Step 3

Create:

```text
/etc/nginx/nginx.conf
```

---

#### Step 4

Start Nginx.

```bash
nginx -g 'daemon off;'
```

### Why "daemon off"?

Keeps Nginx running in the foreground.

Docker containers stop when the main process exits.

---

# Complete Build Flow

```text
Stage 1 (Weaver)
│
├── Copy Telemetry Schema
├── Copy Templates
├── Generate Markdown
└── Generate Schema Package
        │
        ▼
Stage 2 (MkDocs)
│
├── Install MkDocs
├── Copy Documentation
├── Build Static Website
└── Create Site Files
        │
        ▼
Stage 3 (Nginx)
│
├── Copy Website Files
├── Generate Nginx Configuration
├── Start Nginx
└── Serve Documentation Website
```

---

# Summary

## Stage 1 – Weaver

1. Reads telemetry schema.
2. Reads documentation templates.
3. Generates Markdown documentation.
4. Packages schema files.

## Stage 2 – MkDocs

5. Installs MkDocs and Material theme.
6. Copies generated documentation.
7. Builds a static website.

## Stage 3 – Nginx

8. Uses lightweight Nginx image.
9. Runs as non-root user.
10. Serves generated website files.
11. Creates Nginx configuration dynamically.
12. Hosts the telemetry documentation website.

### Final Result

The container automatically converts OpenTelemetry schema definitions into a fully functional documentation website and serves it through Nginx, making telemetry information easy to browse and search.
